module std.goal_assessment

import std.types { List }
import std.algebra { FreeMonoid, Empty, Cons, FreeSemigroup }

// THE ONE SUBJECT-AGNOSTIC SHAPE FOR "IS THE WORLD AS IT WAS DECLARED TO BE".
//
// This replaces gunbc.world_converge, which fused four separable facts into one handler and could
// therefore only answer honestly for a subject that never actuates. The replacement keeps the part
// that was right -- one shape, bound per subject, so a third subject adds a binding rather than
// widening this authority -- and separates the three distinctions the fused shape destroyed.
//
// ONE: OBSERVATION AND ASSESSMENT ARE TWO LAYERS, NOT ONE COPRODUCT. "No usable snapshot was
// obtained" and "the snapshot is assessable and this subject within it is unknown" are different
// facts with different remedies, and the second is not representable at all if the first is the
// only unknown arm. Collapsing them loses partial evidence, and -- the reason it is a safety
// matter rather than a modelling one -- it licenses treating an UNOBSERVABLE member as an ABSENT
// one, which is what makes an unreadable subject look like a subject that needs creating.
//
// TWO: A DIVERGENCE IS NONEMPTY BY CONSTRUCTION. `WorldDiverged { differences: [] }` was writable
// in the shape this replaces: a known divergence naming nothing, which is agreement wearing the
// other variant, and every consumer that folded over the list would have found nothing to do while
// the value said the world had drifted. `FreeSemigroup` (std.algebra) has no empty inhabitant, so
// the state has no constructor rather than a validator -- DESIGN section 4b rung four. It is
// deliberately NOT a fresh `NonEmptyList`: std.algebra already owns the free semigroup over T as
// the monoid's other identity, and its own grounding note withholds that alias precisely so one
// name never resolves to two declarations (DESIGN section 3).
//
// THREE: THE OBSERVER CANNOT SEE THE GOAL, AND THIS IS A SHAPE RATHER THAN A CONVENTION. An
// observer handed the desired state can omit what the caller did not ask about, and an observation
// shaped by what someone hoped to find cannot falsify the hope -- it is the fabricated-plausible-
// output failure moved one stage earlier, where nothing downstream can detect it.
//
// THE FIRST CUT STATED THIS SENTENCE WHILE THE TYPE ADMITTED ITS OPPOSITE. `observe` took
// `(Subject, Scope)`, and a subject is exactly where a goal travels: gunbc.spark.fabric_switch
// bound an observer that matched on `subject.intent`, so the desired mode selected which lanes were
// read -- with the module's own note claiming the separation held. A guarantee carried only by
// prose is one binding away from being false, and it was false in two of the three bindings that
// existed. So the observer now receives the SUBJECT and an `ObservationRequest`, and no goal: it
// has no goal-typed parameter to read, so the desired side is unconstructible in its argument list.
// (An earlier wording here said it receives a request "and NOTHING ELSE". That was written before
// the subject was restored to the observer -- goal-blind is not subject-blind -- and it went false
// in the edit that restored it. It is corrected rather than quietly dropped: this module's whole
// subject is prose outliving the code it described.)
//
// WHAT THIS DOES NOT CLOSE, stated so the rung is not read as higher than it is. THERE ARE THREE
// ROUTES BY WHICH A GOAL CAN REACH A READ, and this type closes exactly one of them.
//
//   PARAMETER -- structurally impossible. There is no goal-typed argument for an observer to read.
//
//   CAPTURE -- mitigated by review. A binding may pass a lambda that closes over the goal lexically
//     and shape the read by it, and no type here can see that.
//
//   GOAL-DERIVED REQUEST -- mitigated by review. The `request` is constructed by the CALLER, so a
//     caller may compute it from the goal and hand the observer a scope the desire chose. The
//     observer stays honest and the read is still shaped by the goal, one level up. This is not
//     hypothetical: gunbc.spark.fabric_switch_assessment did exactly this in the change that
//     introduced the split -- its request was the intent's own interface list, so an undecided
//     intent produced an empty snapshot BECAUSE OF THE GOAL. Closing the parameter route while the
//     request was still goal-derived renamed the mechanism rather than removing it. That binding
//     now derives its request from the absence of a capture instead, but the TYPE does not force
//     that and cannot: a request is by nature caller-chosen.
//
// So the honest rung for goal-blindness is STRUCTURAL against the parameter and MITIGATED BY REVIEW
// against both capture and goal-derived requests. test.claim.goal_assessment_witness holds the
// capture residual as an executing fact.
//
// NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER, and it is one lens covering both residuals: a reader over the `Node` tree that
// refuses an `observe` argument, or a `request` argument, whose free variables include the goal
// bound at the same `inspect_goal` call site. Both residuals are the same shape -- a goal reaching
// the read through a value rather than a parameter -- so neither climbs until that lens exists.
//
// WHAT THIS MODULE DELIBERATELY DOES NOT CARRY, stated so its absence reads as a boundary and not
// an oversight. There is no `reconcile_once`: no planner, no admission, no actuator, and no
// post-actuation re-observation. Those are the effectful half of the same protocol, and the reason
// they are not here is DESIGN section 3c rather than schedule -- their only honest first consumer
// is the fleet admission spine (gunbc.fleet_converge_plan: generation, held lease, observed
// baseline, compare-and-set), and a protocol shipped with a single hand-fitted adapter is exactly
// how the shape this module replaces came to have an apply stage that no corpus path ever ran.
// TRIGGER: the effectful half lands when that spine binds it, and not before.
//
// There is likewise no goal REVISION carried in the result. The law it would serve is real -- a
// receipt should say which revision of the goal was assessed, or a stale controller reports
// agreement it never checked -- but this module's consumers today declare their goal as corpus
// data, so a revision parameter would be filled with a placeholder at every call site: modelling
// with no consumer, which section 3c reds. TRIGGER: the plan basis, where a revision is what the
// compare-and-set is fenced against and therefore has a real inhabitant.

// THE THREE NEAREST NEIGHBOURS, AND WHY THIS IS NOT A FOURTH SPELLING OF ANY OF THEM. Each already
// owns a question this module deliberately does not ask, so a reader arriving with one of them in
// mind can tell immediately whether they are in the right module (DESIGN section 3).
//
//   std.claim_evidence ClaimAssessment -- the admitted evidence links for a named CLAIM and their
//     support/challenge standing. Its subject is an argument, not a world. A domain may well
//     produce this module's Evidence out of claim assessments; it is not obliged to route an
//     ordinary observation through the claim machinery to be assessed against a goal.
//   std.claim_evidence ClaimRequirementReadiness -- whether ONE required claim is ready after
//     evidence admission. A readiness, not a comparison.
//   std.upsert_decision ObservationVerdict / UpsertClassification -- a POINT classification of one
//     observation, paired with a remedy (Noop | Apply | Refuse). That pairing is exactly what this
//     module refuses to make: a GoalAssessment is remedy-free, and choosing what to do about a
//     deviation belongs to a planner that does not exist here yet.
//
// So the fact this carrier owns, stated once: the REMEDY-FREE AGGREGATE RELATION between one
// declared goal and one established observation of the world it describes.

// The first layer. `ObservationRefused` means NO usable snapshot was produced for the requested
// scope -- not that the subject is absent within one. An ESTABLISHED ABSENCE is a fact INSIDE the
// snapshot and belongs in `Observed`: "the ruleset does not exist" is something the reader saw,
// and routing it to a refusal would make an observed absence indistinguishable from a failed read
// -- the same collapse, one layer down, that this module's two layers exist to prevent.
type ObservationAttempt<Observed, ObserveRefusal>
  = ObservationEstablished { observed: Observed }
  | ObservationRefused { cause: ObserveRefusal }

// The second layer, and the module's carrier.
//
// GoalDiverged versus GoalIndeterminate is the distinction the whole module exists for. Diverged
// means enough is KNOWN to prove a mismatch. Indeterminate means a fact the assessment required
// was not established -- and it still carries whatever mismatches WERE proved, because discarding
// them would make an unknown fact suppress a known one. The two are not orderable into one
// "unhealthy" bucket: their owners differ, and generic remediation is admissible over the first
// and refused over the second.
//
// GoalAssessmentRefused is neither. It means the inputs cannot be honestly compared at all --
// duplicate identities in the expected roster, a subject paired to the wrong host, a desired state
// this projection cannot express. An assessment that returns a verdict over an ill-formed question
// set certifies exactness over a denominator nobody checked (std.observation_completeness carries
// that argument in full for the join case).
//
// AND IT IS NOW CONSTRUCTED, WHICH RETIRES THE FRONTIER THAT STOOD HERE. This arm was a DECLARED
// FRONTIER under DESIGN section 3c -- no binding minted it, so it was admissible only with its
// trigger stated beside it, and that trigger was a NAMED CONSUMER rather than a date:
// product.fan_tach_health, which computed the same fact in its own vocabulary. That module is now
// bound to this algebra and mints this arm on three causes -- a host paired to another host's
// windows, a roster naming one header twice, a collector answering twice for one endpoint -- each
// of them an ill-formed question set rather than an unhealthy fan. The frontier note is deleted
// rather than left standing, because section 4b(4) retires the lower-rung machinery a climb
// obsoletes and a note describing a state that no longer holds is worse than none: it would cite a
// constructor that no longer exists and understate the arm's reach.
//
// Two bindings still reach it never, and that is a property of their subjects rather than of this
// arm: gunbc.repo_ruleset instantiates AssessRefusal at v2.std.cardinality Never because no state
// of that subject leaves its inputs incomparable, and the realization-selection witness never mints
// it either. A parameter instantiated at Never is a binding declaring the arm unreachable FOR
// ITSELF, which is the honest use of it.
//
// The distinction the arm keeps is why it would be wrong to drop it even with no consumer: "the
// world differs" and "the question was malformed" have different owners, and a coproduct that
// cannot express the second forces a binding with an ill-formed input to file it as a divergence.
// AND THERE IS DELIBERATELY NO `is_satisfied` PREDICATE OVER THIS CARRIER, which is the design
// rather than an omission. A `Bool` here is a one-bit summary of four arms whose whole point is
// that they are not orderable into two: the summary's false side silently merges "proved to
// differ", "not fully established" and "could not be compared", which have three different owners
// and three different remedies. That is the conflation the arms above exist to prevent, so
// offering it as a convenience would hand every consumer the defect back.
//
// Every real consumer matches instead, and it must -- the non-satisfied arms carry the payloads it
// has to report. A consumer that genuinely wants only the fixed-point question is one `match` arm
// away from it, and writing that arm is where it decides what the other three mean to it.
type GoalAssessment<Evidence, Deviation, UnknownFact, AssessRefusal>
  = GoalSatisfied { evidence: Evidence }
  | GoalDiverged { deviations: FreeSemigroup<Deviation> }
  | GoalIndeterminate {
      known_deviations: List<Deviation>,
      unknowns: FreeSemigroup<UnknownFact>,
    }
  | GoalAssessmentRefused { cause: AssessRefusal }

// The two layers joined. Observation refusal stays OUTSIDE GoalAssessment rather than becoming a
// fifth arm of it: an assessment is a statement about a snapshot, and there is no snapshot here to
// make one about.
//
// THE SUBJECT IS CARRIED IN BOTH ARMS, including the refused one. The shape this replaces did the
// same, and a replacement that dropped it would have erased a distinction rather than completed a
// migration: a refusal that does not say WHAT it is about is a diagnostic missing its located half
// (DESIGN section 5), and the refused arm is exactly where a reader has least context to supply it.
//
// THE GOAL AND THE REQUEST ARE CARRIED BESIDE THE SUBJECT, for the reason the subject is carried: a
// result that does not say WHAT WAS WANTED, and WHAT WAS ACTUALLY READ TO FIND OUT, is only
// partly located. Carrying the goal also lets a consumer report the comparison without reaching
// back to whatever declared the desire, which is where a stale goal gets substituted for the
// assessed one. Carrying the request is what makes a refusal say which read was attempted rather
// than only which subject it concerned.
type GoalInspection<Subject, Goal, ObservationRequest, Observed, Evidence, Deviation, UnknownFact, ObserveRefusal, AssessRefusal>
  = GoalInspected {
      subject: Subject,
      goal: Goal,
      request: ObservationRequest,
      observed: Observed,
      assessment: GoalAssessment<Evidence, Deviation, UnknownFact, AssessRefusal>,
    }
  | GoalObservationRefused {
      subject: Subject,
      goal: Goal,
      request: ObservationRequest,
      cause: ObserveRefusal,
    }

// THE ROOT. Unobservable is guarded first and the assessor is never reached, for the same reason
// the shape this replaces guarded it first: an unreadable subject has no deviations to invent, and
// an assessor called on a subject nobody could read can only answer about a fabricated snapshot.
//
// It takes the two operations directly rather than through a handler record. The record bought
// nothing here -- there is no dispatch over it and no second implementation selected at runtime --
// while it cost every binding a named type alias and every witness a data row to reach the very
// functions it already had.
//
// THE THREE INPUTS ARE THREE FACTS. `subject` is WHO is being asked about; `goal` is WHAT WAS
// WANTED; `request` is WHAT TO READ. The first cut fused the second and third into the subject,
// which is how a goal reached an observer while the note above said it could not.
//
// THE OBSERVER IS GOAL-BLIND, NOT SUBJECT-BLIND, and the difference is the whole reason it receives
// two arguments rather than one. Handing it only the request would make `subject` a LABEL ON THE
// RESULT rather than the thing observed: the receipt could name subject A while the request read
// subject B, and nothing in the shape would object. The observer needs the subject precisely
// because it is what it must go and read. What it must not receive is the DESIRE, because an
// observation shaped by the hope cannot falsify it.
//
// THE ASSESSOR DOES NOT RECEIVE THE SUBJECT, and the asymmetry is deliberate rather than an
// oversight. The observer needs it because it must go and read that subject -- gunbc.repo_ruleset's
// observer builds its request path from `subject.owner` and `subject.name`, which is the consumer
// that earns the parameter. An assessor has no such use: it compares a goal against a snapshot, and
// the identity it would want for a located refusal is already carried by `GoalInspection` in BOTH
// arms, so taking it again would be a second copy of a fact the result already holds.
//
// AN EARLIER CUT DID PASS IT, with a sentence here claiming a refusal cause should not have to
// reach the identity through a closure. No assessor in the corpus read it -- five bindings, five
// signatures, zero uses -- so the parameter was section 3c dangling and the sentence asserted a use
// that did not exist. Recorded rather than silently reverted: this module's subject is prose
// outliving the code, and this is where it recurred.
fn inspect_goal<Subject, Goal, ObservationRequest, Observed, Evidence, Deviation, UnknownFact, ObserveRefusal, AssessRefusal>(
  subject: Subject,
  goal: Goal,
  request: ObservationRequest,
  observe: fn(Subject, ObservationRequest) -> ObservationAttempt<Observed, ObserveRefusal>,
  assess: fn(Goal, Observed) -> GoalAssessment<Evidence, Deviation, UnknownFact, AssessRefusal>,
) -> GoalInspection<Subject, Goal, ObservationRequest, Observed, Evidence, Deviation, UnknownFact, ObserveRefusal, AssessRefusal> {
  match observe(subject, request) {
    ObservationRefused { cause: cause } => GoalObservationRefused {
      subject: subject,
      goal: goal,
      request: request,
      cause: cause,
    }
    ObservationEstablished { observed: observed } => GoalInspected {
      subject: subject,
      goal: goal,
      request: request,
      observed: observed,
      assessment: assess(goal, observed),
    }
  }
}

// THE MINT THAT MAKES THE EMPTY DIVERGENCE UNCONSTRUCTIBLE AT THE CALL SITE.
//
// Every assessor arrives at the same fork: it has folded its subject into a list of proved
// mismatches, and an empty list means agreement. Written out at each binding that is
// `if length(ds) == 0 { satisfied } else { diverged { ds } }` -- and the else arm is where the
// empty divergence gets written, because nothing in the type stops a later edit from reaching it
// with the empty list. Here the list is DESTRUCTURED, so the divergent arm holds a head it was
// handed: agreement and divergence are decided by the same match that supplies the payload, and
// they cannot disagree.
fn assess_by_deviations<Evidence, Deviation, UnknownFact, AssessRefusal>(
  evidence: Evidence,
  deviations: FreeMonoid<Deviation>,
) -> GoalAssessment<Evidence, Deviation, UnknownFact, AssessRefusal> {
  match deviations {
    Empty => GoalSatisfied { evidence: evidence }
    Cons { head: first, tail: rest } => GoalDiverged {
      deviations: FreeSemigroup { head: first, tail: rest },
    }
  }
}

// The same mint for the indeterminate fork, and it is a SEPARATE function rather than a flag on
// the one above because the precedence is the whole point: unknowns win over deviations. An
// assessment that proved two mismatches and failed to establish a third fact is indeterminate, not
// divergent -- reporting it as divergent would licence remediation over a subject whose state is
// partly unread, and reporting it as satisfied-with-notes would licence nothing at all.
fn assess_by_deviations_and_unknowns<Evidence, Deviation, UnknownFact, AssessRefusal>(
  evidence: Evidence,
  deviations: FreeMonoid<Deviation>,
  unknowns: FreeMonoid<UnknownFact>,
) -> GoalAssessment<Evidence, Deviation, UnknownFact, AssessRefusal> {
  match unknowns {
    Empty => assess_by_deviations(evidence: evidence, deviations: deviations)
    Cons { head: first, tail: rest } => GoalIndeterminate {
      known_deviations: deviations,
      unknowns: FreeSemigroup { head: first, tail: rest },
    }
  }
}
